---
import BaseLayout from '../layouts/BaseLayout.astro';
import { site } from '../data/site-config';
import { SITES } from '../lib/sites';
import { breadcrumbJsonLd, organizationJsonLd, serviceJsonLd, faqPageJsonLd } from '../lib/seo';

const canonical = `${SITES.main}/oem/`;

const faqs = [
  {
    q: 'Does an OEM order require my own brand?',
    a: 'No. OEM describes who controls the product specification, not whose name is on the box. You can take an OEM product in neutral packaging and sell it under your own label later, or combine a custom product with printed packaging in one order. What separates OEM from private label is that the product itself — raw material, shape, dimension, grade — is made to your specification rather than selected from the catalogue.',
  },
  {
    q: 'What sets the minimum order for OEM charcoal?',
    a: 'Three things, and they do not move together: the smallest run the factory will schedule, the cost and setup of any custom mould or die, and — where packaging is printed — the print run. Public supplier listings commonly describe roughly one tonne for stock packaging and a full 20 ft container once boxes are printed. A custom shape can add a tooling-driven minimum of its own. Only the written quotation binds the figure.',
  },
  {
    q: 'Who owns the mould or die used for a custom shape?',
    a: 'Whoever paid for it, if the paperwork says so — which is why it has to be written down. Before you approve the sample, agree four points: who pays for the tool, who holds it, whether it can be transferred or scrapped at your request, and who is responsible for wear and maintenance. Record the tool identity, cavity count and condition in a tooling annex to the purchase order. Without that clause, possession decides the argument.',
  },
  {
    q: 'Can I change the specification after the golden sample is signed?',
    a: 'Yes, but the change restarts the approval. A new dimension, tolerance, moisture limit or material changes the measurement record, and usually the price and schedule with it. Re-issue the specification with a new version number, take a new sample, and measure it against the new limits. Changes made after production has started create mixed lots, because the earlier output was built to the old specification and cannot be re-labelled into the new one.',
  },
  {
    q: 'What happens if the first production batch misses the specification?',
    a: 'That depends on the acceptance rule you agreed before the run existed. Decide in advance whether a failed result means re-inspection, sorting, rework, replacement of the lot, or a price adjustment — and who pays for the remedy. Keep a retained sample from both the approved golden sample and the disputed lot so the comparison is physical rather than verbal. A specification without a remedy clause is a description, not a contract term.',
  },
];

const jsonLd = [
  breadcrumbJsonLd([
    { name: 'Home', url: `${SITES.main}/` },
    { name: 'OEM', url: canonical },
  ]),
  organizationJsonLd({
    id: SITES.main,
    name: 'Charcoal Hub',
    description: site.description,
  }),
  serviceJsonLd({
    id: canonical,
    name: 'OEM Charcoal Manufacturing',
    serviceType: 'OEM charcoal manufacturing',
    description: 'OEM charcoal manufacturing to a buyer-held specification — custom raw material, shape, dimension, grade and packaging, with tooling ownership terms, sample approval and pre-shipment inspection.',
    providerId: SITES.main,
  }),
  faqPageJsonLd({ id: `${canonical}#faq`, faqs }),
];
---

<BaseLayout
  site={site}
  title="OEM Charcoal Manufacturing — Custom Specification"
  description="OEM charcoal manufacturing explained: how a custom specification is written, who owns moulds and print plates, and the inspection gates before shipment."
  canonical={canonical}
  jsonLd={jsonLd}
  breadcrumbs={[{ name: 'Home', url: '/' }, { name: 'OEM', url: '/oem/' }]}
>
  <div class="page-title container">
    <h1>OEM Charcoal Manufacturing</h1>
    <p class="lede">
      OEM means the product is manufactured to a specification you hold, rather than picked from a
      catalogue. That shifts the work from choosing a product to writing a specification the factory
      can hit, deciding who owns the tooling, and proving the run matched — this page covers those
      three things in the order they cause problems.
    </p>
    <div class="page-title-media">
      <img src="/images/page-manufacturing.webp" alt="Charcoal briquette production line with press and conveyor — conceptual illustration" width="1600" height="900" loading="eager" />
    </div>
  </div>

  <section class="section">
    <div class="container">
      <h2>OEM and Private Label: Where the Line Sits</h2>
      <div class="prose">
        <p>
          The two terms are used loosely in the charcoal trade, and the difference is not the logo on
          the box. Private label takes a product that already exists in the catalogue and changes the
          packaging, the artwork and the commercial terms. OEM changes the product: a different raw
          material, a shape that is not a standard cube, a size outside the usual 25 / 26 / 27 mm
          classes, a tighter tolerance, or a grade written to your limits instead of the supplier's.
        </p>
        <p>
          Most real orders mix the two. A custom product usually still ships in a printed box, so the
          tooling questions of OEM and the artwork questions of private label both apply. What
          matters is knowing which half of the order is fixed by the factory's existing line and
          which half has to be created for you.
        </p>
      </div>
      <div class="table-wrap">
        <table>
          <thead>
            <tr><th>Question</th><th>Private label</th><th>OEM</th></tr>
          </thead>
          <tbody>
            <tr><td>What changes</td><td class="spec-value">Packaging, artwork, brand</td><td class="spec-value">Product: raw material, shape, dimension, grade, pack format</td></tr>
            <tr><td>Where the product comes from</td><td class="spec-value">A catalogue record you select</td><td class="spec-value">A specification written for the order</td></tr>
            <tr><td>Tooling involved</td><td class="spec-value">Print plates only</td><td class="spec-value">Mould or die, plus print plates if printed</td></tr>
            <tr><td>What sets the minimum</td><td class="spec-value">Charcoal run and print run</td><td class="spec-value">Tooling setup and run length, then print</td></tr>
            <tr><td>What the sample proves</td><td class="spec-value">Pack and print match the approved version</td><td class="spec-value">Dimensions, mass, moisture and burn match the written limits</td></tr>
            <tr><td>What can drift on reorder</td><td class="spec-value">Artwork version, board supply</td><td class="spec-value">Tool wear, drying conditions, material lot</td></tr>
          </tbody>
        </table>
      </div>
      <div class="prose">
        <p>
          Two related terms are worth separating while the order is still a draft. White label is an
          off-the-shelf product sold under your brand — closer to private label than to OEM. ODM means
          the supplier designs the product and you brand it, so the specification is theirs and you
          are choosing from their development. Neither is better; they put the engineering
          responsibility in different hands, and the price and the risk follow it.
        </p>
        <p>
          If the product does not need to change, the
          <a href="/private-label/">private label page</a> is the shorter path: you select a
          recorded size class, approve artwork against a dieline, and the charcoal stays the same. The
          full comparison of white label, private label, OEM and ODM sits in the
          <a href="/private-label-oem-charcoal-guide/">private label and OEM guide</a>.
        </p>
      </div>

      <h2>What the Specification Has to Control</h2>
      <div class="prose">
        <p>
          An OEM order asks a supplier to hit a target instead of shipping a catalogue item, so the
          specification sheet is the product. Nine elements make it enforceable. Each one exists
          because a factory can satisfy the words while missing the intent if the element is left out.
        </p>
      </div>
      <div class="table-wrap">
        <table>
          <thead>
            <tr><th>Element</th><th>What to write</th><th>Why it can be checked</th></tr>
          </thead>
          <tbody>
            <tr><td>Product identity</td><td class="spec-value">Raw material and form — e.g. carbonised coconut shell, pressed cube</td><td class="spec-value">A COA or a burn test contradicts the wrong carbon immediately</td></tr>
            <tr><td>Dimension and tolerance</td><td class="spec-value">Nominal size plus a permitted band, e.g. 25 mm ±0.5 mm</td><td class="spec-value">Calipers on a random sample turn the claim into a number</td></tr>
            <tr><td>Sampling plan</td><td class="spec-value">How many pieces, from which lot, measured with what</td><td class="spec-value">A tolerance without a sampling rule cannot be enforced</td></tr>
            <tr><td>Piece mass or count</td><td class="spec-value">Pieces per kilogram at a stated moisture, or grams per piece</td><td class="spec-value">Count and net weight can both be true only if one is derived from the other</td></tr>
            <tr><td>Moisture</td><td class="spec-value">Limit plus basis — as-received or dry</td><td class="spec-value">A percentage without a basis is not comparable between labs</td></tr>
            <tr><td>Ash, fixed carbon, volatiles</td><td class="spec-value">Value plus test method designation</td><td class="spec-value">The method decides the number; the same sample reads differently across methods</td></tr>
            <tr><td>Burn and ignition</td><td class="spec-value">Duration plus conditions: piece size, airflow, lighting method</td><td class="spec-value">Burn time without conditions is marketing, not a specification</td></tr>
            <tr><td>Packaging</td><td class="spec-value">Format, board, net weight, piece count, barrier</td><td class="spec-value">The pack is part of the product once it reaches retail</td></tr>
            <tr><td>Acceptance rule</td><td class="spec-value">What happens on a miss: re-inspect, sort, rework, replace, adjust</td><td class="spec-value">A limit without a remedy is an argument waiting for a shipment</td></tr>
          </tbody>
        </table>
      </div>
      <div class="prose">
        <p>
          Three habits make the sheet usable. First, separate a <em>limit</em> from a
          <em>typical</em> value: “≤ 3.5% ash, typical 3.0%” tells the factory what fails and what to
          aim for, while a single number hides which one it is. Second, attach a method and a basis to
          every measured value — the
          <a href={`${SITES.data}/specifications/`} target="_blank" rel="noopener">specification pages</a>
          define each metric, and the
          <a href={`${SITES.testing}/tests/`} target="_blank" rel="noopener">method reference</a>
          lists the standards behind them. Third, state the sampling point: a moisture reading taken
          at the press, at the packing line and at the destination port are three different numbers
          for one batch.
        </p>
        <p>
          What the sheet cannot do is invent evidence. A size class is a nominal dimension, not a
          chemistry record — the 26 mm and 27 mm records carry piece counts and dimensions from a
          public table but no batch ash or fixed carbon. If your brand needs a chemistry claim, it has
          to come from a supplier datasheet or a laboratory report for the batch you buy. The
          <a href="/coconut-shell-charcoal-guide/">coconut shell specification guide</a> explains how
          to read those records without over-claiming them.
        </p>
      </div>

      <h2>Tooling: Moulds, Dies and Who Holds Them</h2>
      <div class="prose">
        <p>
          A standard cube runs on a mould the factory already has. A custom profile — a different
          footprint, a rounded edge, a larger format, a hole through the piece — needs a die made for
          the order. That die is a physical asset with a life, and its ownership is a commercial term,
          not a detail to settle after the first shipment.
        </p>
        <p>
          Assume the tool wears. Pressing abrasive carbon against a die changes the cavity over a long
          run, and the change shows up as dimension drift rather than a sudden failure. Ask for
          first-article and last-article measurements from the same run, so you can see whether the
          tool held the tolerance from the beginning of the lot to the end. On a reorder, ask when the
          tool was last inspected and whether it was replaced.
        </p>
        <p>
          Four points belong in writing before the tool is cut:
        </p>
        <ul>
          <li><strong>Who pays</strong> — tooling is normally a one-time charge, separate from the unit price. It is not a per-piece cost, and it should not quietly reappear in a later quotation as one.</li>
          <li><strong>Who holds it</strong> — the factory, the toolmaker, or a third party. Possession decides who can use it for another buyer.</li>
          <li><strong>Whether it can move</strong> — if you paid for the tool, ask for a written statement that it is yours and can be transferred or scrapped at your request. A tool that cannot leave the factory is leverage, not an asset.</li>
          <li><strong>Who maintains it</strong> — wear, re-polishing and replacement, and at whose cost. This is the clause that matters on the third reorder, not the first.</li>
        </ul>
        <p>
          Record the tool identity in a tooling annex: a photograph, the cavity count, the date of
          manufacture and the condition at handover. “The mould we made for you” is not an identifier.
        </p>
        <p>
          Printed packaging carries a parallel question. Print plates are commonly charged to the
          buyer once and held by the converter, which means a later switch of packaging supplier may
          require new plates. Artwork ownership is a separate matter from plate ownership: agree that
          the design files remain yours and get the dieline and the approved file back in an editable
          format. The <a href="/packaging/">packaging page</a> covers the construction side — board,
          barrier, palletisation — and the <a href="/private-label/">private label page</a> walks
          through the artwork gate in sequence.
        </p>
      </div>

      <h2>From Drawing to Golden Sample</h2>
      <div class="prose">
        <p>
          A custom run is approved in stages, and each stage answers a different question. Skipping
          one does not save time; it moves the failure later, where it costs more.
        </p>
        <ol>
          <li><strong>Feasibility sample</strong> — can this specification be made at all, and at what rough volume? It tests the drawing against the line. It is not an approval and should not be treated as one.</li>
          <li><strong>Development sample</strong> — a made-to-specification piece you can burn, measure and show to your market. This is where a dimension that looked reasonable on paper turns out to be awkward in the hand.</li>
          <li><strong>Golden sample</strong> — the signed reference for production. For an OEM order it must travel with its measurement record: the specification version, the measured dimensions, piece mass, moisture and the test conditions behind any burn figure. The object alone records what it looks like, not what it is.</li>
          <li><strong>Production sample</strong> — drawn from the actual run, not made separately by hand. Comparing it with the golden sample is the check that the line, not the sample bench, can hold the specification.</li>
        </ol>
        <p>
          The approval record is short and worth more than the sample: sample ID, date, specification
          version, the measured values with their method, a photograph, and the name of the person who
          approved it on each side. Keep one copy with the purchase order and one with the inspection
          file. If a dispute starts, this record is the definition of what was agreed.
        </p>
        <p>
          Samples travel through the <a href="/request-sample/">sample request page</a> so the
          product, size, specification version and destination are recorded together. A sample sent
          without its specification is a demonstration, and a demonstration cannot be inspected
          against.
        </p>
      </div>

      <h2>Inspection Gates for a Custom Run</h2>
      <div class="prose">
        <p>
          Standard production is checked against a catalogue description. A custom run is checked
          against a document you wrote, which makes the gates both more necessary and easier to
          define. Four gates cover most of the risk.
        </p>
      </div>
      <div class="table-wrap">
        <table>
          <thead>
            <tr><th>Gate</th><th>What is checked</th><th>Record that proves it</th></tr>
          </thead>
          <tbody>
            <tr><td>Pre-production</td><td class="spec-value">Material lot, tool identity, signed specification version, approved artwork</td><td class="spec-value">Signed specification sheet and approved proof</td></tr>
            <tr><td>First article</td><td class="spec-value">Dimensions, piece mass, moisture and appearance from the start of the run</td><td class="spec-value">First-article measurement report</td></tr>
            <tr><td>In-process</td><td class="spec-value">Dimension drift, piece mass, moisture at the packing line, cracks and delamination</td><td class="spec-value">Line check log with sample size and result</td></tr>
            <tr><td>Pre-shipment</td><td class="spec-value">Count, dimension sample, moisture, breakage, carton marks, pallet condition</td><td class="spec-value">Inspection report and retained sample</td></tr>
          </tbody>
        </table>
      </div>
      <div class="prose">
        <p>
          The first article is the gate that a catalogue order does not need. It catches a tool that
          was cut to the wrong dimension before a tonne of product exists, and it costs one shift
          rather than one lot. The in-process check exists because a run can start inside tolerance and
          finish outside it; sampling only at the end tells you the average of a drift, not where the
          drift went.
        </p>
        <p>
          Pre-shipment inspection for a custom order should reuse the golden sample physically. Put
          the approved piece beside the sampled piece and measure both, rather than reading a
          specification and judging by eye. The retained sample — one sealed unit from the lot, kept
          by both sides — is what makes a later complaint answerable. The
          <a href={`${SITES.knowledge}/knowledge/pre-shipment-inspection-charcoal/`} target="_blank" rel="noopener">pre-shipment inspection guide</a>
          sets out the checks in the order they are usually run.
        </p>
        <p>
          Documents travel with the goods, not after them: commercial invoice, packing list that
          matches carton and pallet marks, and a certificate of analysis. Read the COA for what it
          actually is — a laboratory report and a seller's declaration look similar and mean different
          things, which the
          <a href="/charcoal-testing-coa-guide/">testing and COA guide</a> explains. Charcoal moves
          under UN 1361, Class 4.2, so the dangerous-goods declaration and safety data sheet belong in
          the same file, and destination rules may add a certificate of origin or label requirements.
        </p>
      </div>

      <h2>Volume, Lead Time and Container Arithmetic</h2>
      <div class="prose">
        <p>
          OEM volume is usually discussed in containers because the tooling and print steps make small
          runs expensive per unit. As a public planning reference, a 20 ft container of coconut shell
          hookah charcoal loads at roughly 14.4 tonnes and a 40 ft high-cube at roughly 23.4 tonnes
          before retail packaging and pallets take their share of the volume. Those figures describe
          the cargo, not a quotation.
        </p>
        <p>
          Lead time splits into two parts that are often conflated: tooling and sampling, then
          production. The first depends on the die and how many sample rounds the specification needs;
          the second is commonly quoted at 15–25 days after artwork approval for a printed order, as
          an industry reference rather than a commitment. Plan backwards from the shipment date and
          put both halves in the schedule, or the sample rounds will consume the production window.
        </p>
        <p>
          The minimum follows the same logic. A stock-packaging run is commonly described at around
          one tonne; a printed order at a full 20 ft container; a custom mould can add a minimum of
          its own because the setup is charged to the first run. The effective minimum is the largest
          of these, not an average, and the written quotation is the only binding statement. The
          arithmetic behind container-based minimums is in the
          <a href={`${SITES.knowledge}/knowledge/coconut-charcoal-moq/`} target="_blank" rel="noopener">MOQ guide</a>
          and the <a href={`${SITES.knowledge}/knowledge/charcoal-container-loading/`} target="_blank" rel="noopener">container-loading guide</a>.
        </p>
      </div>

      <div class="callout-pending">
        <span class="callout-title">Data pending — what this page does not claim</span>
        <p>
          No factory, capacity, certification, client or price is stated on this page, because none
          is verified in the records. The 25 / 26 / 27 mm piece counts (72 / 64 / 56 per kilogram) are
          supplier-declared size records; the 26 mm and 27 mm chemistry, packaging and MOQ remain
          pending. Tooling charges, minimums, lead times and container loads quoted here are industry
          reference or planning figures, not commitments. An OEM order is quoted against your
          specification, tooling and volume — the written quotation is the only binding statement.
        </p>
      </div>

      <h2>OEM Questions</h2>
      <div class="prose">
        {faqs.map((f) => (
          <div>
            <h3>{f.q}</h3>
            <p>{f.a}</p>
          </div>
        ))}
      </div>

      <div class="cta-banner">
        <h2>Have a drawing or a target specification?</h2>
        <p>
          Send the product, dimension and tolerance, target market and first volume — we will confirm
          what can be quoted and what is still pending.
        </p>
        <a class="btn btn-primary" href="/request-quote/">Start an OEM Project →</a>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>
</BaseLayout>
